--TEST--
Two operands whose static types cannot both hold one value are refused before anything runs, `rule:expressions/disjoint-comparison-refused`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// One row of that ADR's table per comparison, and every one of them is
// reported from the same run: the compiler already knows each answer, so
// none of them is a question the program is allowed to ask.
enum Status: int { Active = 1, Banned = 2 }
enum Tier: int { Low = 1, High = 2 }
class A {
}
class B {
}
string $s = "1";
int $n = 1;
uint $u = 1;
bytes $y = $s as bytes;
Status $e = Status::Active;
Tier $t = Tier::Low;
A $a = new A();
B $b = new B();
echo $s == $n, "\n";
echo $s == $y, "\n";
echo $e == 1, "\n";
echo $a == $b, "\n";
echo $a == null, "\n";
// Each enum is its own domain, so two of them are as disjoint as an enum and
// the integer under it — a `match` on one of the two is the written spelling.
echo $e == $t, "\n";
// The other side of the same bound, and the reason the refusal is a
// disjointness question rather than an identity-of-types one: `int`, `uint`,
// `float` and `decimal` are one numeric domain, so this pairing is not
// refused and the count below stays at the comparisons that are.
echo $n == $u, "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0466]: `string` and `int` are disjoint; no value is both, so this comparison is always false
%A
error: aborting due to 6 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
